 $\definecolor{A}{RGB}{121,96,253}\color{A}\fbox{Solution}$ Denote  $o$  as circumcircle  $\triangle ABC$ . Let  $AX$  be the diameter of  $o$  and  $F$  the foot of  $A$  on  $BC$ .
See that  $AH$  and  $AX$  are symmetric wrt  $A$ -angle bisector (angle chasing) and  $\triangle ADE\cup\lbrace P\rbrace\sim\triangle ABC\cup\lbrace F\rbrace$  in that order, since it's the same construction. Therefore   $$ X\in AP. $$  Let  $Y\neq X$  be the intersection of line  $XH$  with  $o$ .
Observe that  $AH$  is the diameter of circle passing through points  $D,E,Y$ . Moreover quadrilateral  $BCDE$  is cyclic ( $BC$  is the diameter).
Radical axis theorem to circles circumscribed on quadrilaterals  $BCDE$ ,  $ADEY$ ,  $ACBY$  gives that lines  $AY, DE, BC$  are concurrent, say in point  $Z$ .
Radical axis theorem to circles circumscribed on quadrilaterals  $BCPQ$ ,  $APQ$  and circle  $o$  gives that lines  $PQ, BC$  and radical axis of circles  $APQ, o$  are concurrent. But  $PQ, BC$  intersect in point  $Z$ , therefore  $AZ$  is the radical axis of circles  $APQ, ABC$ . Because  $AZ$  intersects  $o$  for the second time in point  $Y$ , we have that  $APQY$  is a cyclic quadrilateral. Hence  $$ \angle APQ=90^\circ\implies \angle AYQ=90^\circ=\angle AYX\implies Y,X,Q\text{ are collinear.} $$  From the definition points  $X,H,Y$  are collinear too. Therefore   $$ X\in QH.\blacksquare $$  [asy]
