Consider each oblong rectangular subfigure. Each empty row/column in these (whichever is perpendicular to the longer sides) necessitates two pawns; one in each of the adjoining  $ n \minus{} k$  x  $ n \minus{} k$  squares. We then divide by two because we go over each square block at most twice, and add  $ \sum_{cyc} n \minus{} k \minus{}$ (number of empty rows/columns in each rectangular box) to account for the pawns which are in the rectangular blocks themselves to get  $ 4(n \minus{} k)$  pawns - everything else cancels out.  

Motivation: Divide each side into  $ k$  and  $ n\minus{}k$  blocks from the left and the right (pretty obvious approach), then notice that we can use the rectangular boxes to count corners. 

It would seem intuitively that placing pawns along the diagonal of the central box would be less "wasteful" - anyone have a  $ 4(n \minus{} k)$  example/solution with that? I actually tried that approach for quite a while, but it seemed fruitless to me.

I claim that the answer is  $m=4(n-k)$ . To show that it is sufficient, consider the following diagram (thanks, fwolth):
