Of course we're going to solve this by complex bashing. Identify the incircle with the unit circle, so that  $$ |d|=|e|=|f|=1 $$    $$ a = \frac{2ef}{e+f} $$    $$ b = \frac{2df}{d+f} $$    $$ c = \frac{2de}{d+e} $$  Let  $AI$  meet  $\Omega$  again at  $P$ , so that  $$ p = \frac{2def}{(d+e)(d+f)} $$  We claim  $P$  is the desired point of intersection. Indeed, we work backwards and let  $S$  be the second intersection of line  $PD$  with  $\Omega$  -- it will then suffice to show that  $S = T$ . Now the center  $O$  of  $\Omega$  has coordinate  $$ o = \frac{2def(d+e+f)}{(d+e)(d+f)(e+f)} $$  Then  $S$  is the reflection of  $P$  over the line through  $O$  perpendicular to line  $PD$ . So if  $\omega$  is a unit vector in the direction of this line, and if  $s' = s-o$  and  $p' = p-o$ , we have  $$ \frac{s'}{\omega} = \frac{\overline{p'}}{\overline{\omega}} $$    $$ s' = \omega^2\overline{p'} $$  Now  $$ p' = -\frac{2d^2ef}{(d+e)(d+f)(e+f)} $$  so  $$ \overline{p'} = -\frac{2ef}{(d+e)(d+f)(e+f)} $$  And  $$ \frac{\omega}{p-d} \in i\mathbb{R} $$  so  $$ \omega^2 = -\frac{p-d}{\overline{p}-\overline{d}} = -\frac{\frac{2def}{(d+e)(d+f)} - d}{\frac{2d}{(d+e)(d+f)} - \frac1d} = -\frac{d^2(d^2+de+df-ef)}{de+df+ef-d^2} $$  Then  $$ s' = \omega^2\overline{p'} = \frac{2d^2ef(d^2+de+df-ef)}{(d+e)(d+f)(e+f)(de+df+ef-d^2)} $$  and so  $$ s = s' + o = \frac{2def(d^2e+d^2f+de^2+2def+df^2+e^2f+ef^2)}{(d+e)(d+f)(e+f)(de+df+ef-d^2)} = \frac{2def}{de+df+ef-d^2} $$  Now  $P$  lies on  $\Omega$ , on the opposite side of  $BC$  from  $A$ , and  $D$  lies on line  $BC$  and inside  $\Omega$ . Thus  $S$  lies on the same side of line  $BC$  as  $A$ , so it will suffice to show that line  $KS$  is tangent to  $\Omega$ .

Now instead of continuing to work backwards, let's work forwards. The conditions on  $X$  and  $Y$  imply that  $$ |x|=|y|=1 $$  and  $$ \frac{\left(\frac{b-x}{d-x}\right)}{\left(\frac{d-y}{c-y}\right)} \in \mathbb{R} $$    $$ \frac{(2df-dx-fx)(2de-dy-ey)}{(d+e)(d+f)(d-x)(d-y)} = \frac{d^2(2x-d-f)(2y-d-e)}{(d+e)(d+f)(d-x)(d-y)} $$    $$ (2df-dx-fx)(2de-dy-ey) = d^2(2x-d-f)(2y-d-e) $$    $$ 4d^2ef - 2dex(d+f) - 2dfy(d+e) + xy(d+e)(d+f) = 4d^2xy - 2d^2x(d+e) - 2d^2y(d+f) + d^2(d+e)(d+f) $$    $$ xy(3d^2-de-df-ef) - 2dx(d^2-ef) - 2dy(d^2-ef) + d^2(d^2+de+df-3ef) = 0 $$    $$ x + y = \frac{xy(3d^2-de-df-ef) + d^2(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2d(d^2-ef)} $$  Then
\begin{align*}
k &= \frac{d^2(x+y) - 2dxy}{d^2-xy} 
&= \frac{\frac{d^2xy(3d^2-de-df-ef)+d^4(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2d(d^2-ef)} - 2dxy}{d^2-xy} 
&= \frac{d^2xy(-d^2-de-df+3ef) + d^4(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2d(d^2-ef)(d^2-xy)} 
&= \frac{d(d^2-xy)(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2(d^2-ef)(d^2-xy)} 
&= \frac{d(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2(d^2-ef)}
\end{align*}
where we know  $d^2-xy \neq 0$ , or else  $BD = CD$  and  $AB = AC$ , which contradicts the problem statement.

Finally, we have the vector
\begin{align*}
k-s &= \frac{d(d^2+de+df-3ef)}{2(d^2-ef)}  - \frac{2def}{de+df+ef-d^2} 
&= \frac{d\left[(d^2+de+df-3ef)(de+df+ef-d^2) - 4ef(d^2-ef)\right]}{2(d^2-ef)(de+df+ef-d^2)} 
&= \frac{d(-d^4+d^2e^2+2d^2ef+d^2f^2-2de^2f-2def^2+e^2f^2)}{2(d^2-ef)(de+df+ef-d^2)} 
&= -\frac{d(d-e)(d-f)(d^2+de+df-ef)}{2(d^2-ef)(de+df+ef-d^2)}
\end{align*}
