Consider the pencil of lines passing through  $D$ . We prove that the map  $DX \mapsto DY$  is a projective map, and that it is an involution.

For this, we first perform a negative inversion at  $D$  with radius  $\sqrt{DB\cdot DC}$ . Note that  $B \leftrightarrow C$ . Now we also have,
\[ \measuredangle DB^*X^* = - \measuredangle DXB = - \measuredangle CYD = \measuredangle DYC = - \measuredangle DC^*Y^* = \measuredangle Y^*C^*D \implies \measuredangle DCX^* = \measuredangle Y^*BD. \]

This must mean that  $CX^* \cap  BY^*$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $BC$ . Now let  $\omega$  denote the incircle of  $\triangle ABC$  and  $\omega^*$  denote its image under the inversion.

We animate  $X$  on  $\omega$  projectively. Note that  $X \mapsto X^*$  is projective. Now we can unproject the points of  $\omega^*$  through the pencil of lines passing through  $B$  and then project it back onto the perpendicular bisector of  $BC$ . Now we can unproject the points on the perpendicular bisector through the pencil of lines through  $C$  and then project it back onto  $\omega^*$ . This means that  $X^* \mapsto Y^*$  is projective. Now inverting back at  $D$ , we get that  $Y^* \mapsto Y$  is also projective. Thus we conclude that,
\[ X \mapsto X^* \mapsto BX^* \mapsto BX^* \cap \text{ perp bisec} \mapsto CY^* \mapsto Y^* \mapsto Y\quad \text{ via }\quad \omega \mapsto \omega^* \mapsto \mathcal C_B \mapsto \text{perp bisec} \mapsto \mathcal C_C \mapsto \omega^* \mapsto \omega \]
is projective, where  $\mathcal C_{\chi}$  denotes the pencil of lines passing through  $\chi$ .

Now it is easy to see that doing the same thing again on the point  $Y$  gives the image as  $X$ . Thus the map maps  $X\mapsto Y \mapsto X$  and is a projective map. Hence it must be an involution.

It is well known that any involution on  $\mathcal C \mapsto \mathcal C$  must have a point through which all  $XX^*$  lines pass, where  $\mathcal C$  denotes any conic. For our problem, let the lines  $XY$  pass through a fixed point called  $K$ .

In this map, it is easy to note that  $D \mapsto \infty \mapsto B\infty \mapsto \infty \mapsto C\infty \mapsto \infty \mapsto D$ .

Thus  $K$  lies on the tangent at  $D$  to  $\omega$ . Now we can note that  $KX\cdot KY = KD^2$ . Now,
\[ \measuredangle XBC=\measuredangle XBD=\measuredangle BXD + \measuredangle XDB=\measuredangle DYC + \measuredangle XYD = \measuredangle XYC. \]
This gives us that  $XYBC$  is cyclic. Thus we can also get,
\[ \operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(I)}(K) = KD^2 = KX \cdot KY = KB \cdot KC = \operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(ABC)}(K). \]
This means that  $K$  lies on the radical axis of  $\left\{\odot(ABC), \odot(I)\right\}$ . Clearly  $BC$  is not their radical axis, so we can also define  $K$  as the intersection of the radical axis and line  $BC$ .

Let  $L$  be the midpoint of the arc  $\widehat{BC}$  not containing  $A$ . We also redefine  $T = LD\cap \odot(ABC)$ .

Thus it suffices to prove that  $KT$  is tangent to  $\odot(ABC)$ .

Define  $K'$  to be the intersection of the tangent at  $T$  to  $\odot(ABC)$ . We prove that  $K' \equiv K$ . Firstly, note that  $T$  is just the sharky-devil point. So by inverting about a circle with center  $L$  and radius  $LB$ , we get that  $\measuredangle LBT = \measuredangle BDT$ . Now, we have that,
\[ \measuredangle K'TL = \measuredangle TCL = \measuredangle TBL = \measuredangle LDB = \measuredangle TDK'. \]

This gives us that  $K'T = K'D$ . This gives us that,
\[ \operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(ABC)}(K') = K'T^2 = K'D^2 = \operatorname{Pow}_{\odot(I)}(K') \]
which means that  $K'$  lies on the radical axis of  $\left\{\odot(ABC), \odot(I)\right\}$ . Thus this finally means that  $K'$  is also the intersection of the radical axis of their and line  $BC$ .

So finally this implies that  $K' \equiv K$  and we are done. :yoda:

