First,we have  $gcd(d_i,d_j)=1,\quad \forall 1\le i<j\le k$ .
This is because  $d_i|\frac{n}{d_i}+1$ ,so  $gcd(d_i,d_j)\le gcd(d_i,\frac{n}{d_i})=1$ .
In this way,we have  $$ d_i=a_i^2,\quad \forall 1\le i\le k $$  We let  $\overline{a_i^2}=\frac{n}{a_i^2}$ .
So  $$ a_i^2|\overline{a_i^2}+1,\quad \forall 1\le i\le k $$  So we have
\begin{align*}
& \prod_{i=1}^k a_i^2|\prod_{i=1}^k \left (\overline{a_i^2}+1\right ) 
\implies & \prod_{i=1}^k a_i^2|\left (\sum_{i=1}^k \overline{a_i^2}\right )+1
\end{align*}
However,we have
\begin{align*}
& \frac{1}{a_1^2}+\frac{1}{a_2^2}+\cdots +\frac{1}{a_n^2} 
< & \frac{1}{1\times 2}+\frac{1}{2\times 3}+\cdots +\frac{1}{n\times (n+1)} 
< & 1
\end{align*}
Which means  $$  \prod_{i=1}^k a_i^2>\left (\sum_{i=1}^k \overline{a_i^2}\right ) $$  Contradiction!

