**Solution:** Let  $S$  be the unit square and call  $\mathcal{P}$  our partition. Call a *piece of segment* of  $\mathcal{P}$  the segment that connects two intersection points of segments of  $\mathcal{P}$  and call a *whole segment* the largest possible piece of segment that contains an specific side of   $\mathcal{P}$ . We begin with some intuitive observations: Note that, if some whole segment  $a$  of  $\mathcal{P}$  starts on the boundary of  $S$ , then it must ends above another segment  $b$  of  $\mathcal{P}$ . This is because, if  $a$  doesn't end at any other segment of  $\mathcal{P}$ , then, its other endpoint must lie on the boundary of  $S$  and that will mean the line that contains  $a$  doesn't intersect any rectangle of  $\mathcal{P}$  on its interior, which cannot happen. Also, we can point out that no two  whole segments  $a$  and  $b$  share a common endpoint, because if an endpoint  $P$  of  $b$  is also the endpoint of  $a$ , then,  $P$  cannot be at the boundary of any rectangle of  $\mathcal{P}$ , another contradiction.
[asy]
         size(4cm);
         draw((0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle);

         pair A=(0,0.66), P=(0.70,0.66), C=(0.70,0.30);
         label(" $a$ ", midpoint(A--P), dir(90));

        draw(A--P);
        draw(P--C);
        dot(P,red);

        label(" $P$ ",P,dir(45));

        label(" $b$ ", midpoint(P--C), dir(0));
[/asy]

Now, let  $R$  be the rectangle that covers the upper left corner of the square(the *main rectangle* of  $S$ ) and  $a$  be the whole segment that contains its rightmost side, as well  $b$  be the whole segment that contains its bottommost side. By the problem condition, eventually  $a$  and  $b$  must end at the segment of some rectangle of this partition. We also have that at least one among  $a$  and  $b$  must cannot be completely contained on the piece segment of  $R$ , if this happens both will share a common endpoint. WLOG, assume  $b$  doesn't ends at  $a$ . Let  $b$  ends at a whole segment  $s_0\neq a$ . If  $s_0$  does not extend to the uppermost side of  $S$ , then it must end above another  whole segment  $s_1$  of the partition
[asy]
    size(6cm);
    pair A=(0.75,0.675), B=(0.75,0.825);
pair C=(0.70,0.825), D=(0.80,0.825);

draw(A--B);
draw(C--D);

label(" $s_0$ ", midpoint(A--B), dir(0));
label(" $s_1$ ", midpoint(C--D), dir(90));

draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);

draw((0,1)--(0.66,1)--(0.66,0.75)--(0,0.75)--cycle);

draw((0,0.75)--(0.75,0.75));

draw((0.75,0.675)--(0.75,0.825));

draw((0.70,0.825)--(0.80,0.825));
[/asy]
Now, look at the points on the interior of the left part of the  $90^\circ$ -angle formed by the intersection of  $s_0$  and  $s_1$ , which clearly must exist since  $s_0$  and  $s_1$  cannot share endpoints. It must be on the interior of some rectangle of this partition, but, this rectangle cannot have any common side with the boundary of  $S$  and thus we'll be done!

So, suppose  $s_0$  extends to a point on the uppermost side of  $S$ . Hence, remove all the piece segments of the partition at the left of  $s_0$  and at the top of  $b$ . This obviously does not create an interior rectangle and, since  $s_0\neq a$ , at the end of this process, at least one piece of segment of the partition must be removed and we will get a larger main rectangle. Thus, if there isn't a rectangle having no point on the boundary of the square, we will be able to perform this algorithm infinitely many times. But it costs at least one piece of segment of  $\mathcal{P}$ , which is clearly a finite number, hence we get a contradiction! Thus, there must exist such a rectangle.  $\blacksquare$

