  \binom k2 \left(r - \frac{nk}{4(k - 1)}\right) & > n\binom{\frac {kr}n}{2} 
  \frac{k(k - 1)}{2}\left(r - \frac{nk}{4(k - 1)}\right) & > \frac{(kr - n)(kr)}{2n} 
  kr - r - \frac{nk}4 & > \frac{kr^2}{n} - r
  0 & > 4kr^2 - 4krn + n^2k 
  (2r - n)^2& < 0
\end{align*}
which is a contradiction.

We calculate the expected size of the intersection of two subsets. Let  $X_i$  denote the expected number of subsets element  $i$  of  $X$  is in. Then, the expected size of the intersection is just: \[\sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{\tbinom{X_i}{2}}{\tbinom{k}{2}} = \sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{X_i^2-X_i}{2\tbinom{k}{2}} \ge \sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{X_i^2}{k(k-1)} - \dfrac{kr}{k(k-1)} = \sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{X_i^2}{k(k-1)} - \dfrac{r}{k-1}.\] Now, by Cauchy-Schwarz, \[\sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{X_i^2}{k(k-1)} \ge \dfrac{(X_1+X_2+\dots+X_n)^2}{nk(k-1)} \ge \dfrac{kr^2}{n(k-1)}.\] Hence, \[\sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{\tbinom{X_i}{2}}{\tbinom{k}{2}} \ge \dfrac{kr^2}{n(k-1)}-\dfrac{r}{k-1}.\] Now, we wish to show that:
